      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order yet. The maximum recommended dose of amlodipine for adults is **10 mg once daily**, so 20 mg is double the labeled maximum. Higher doses raise the risk of peripheral edema, hypotension, and reflex tachycardia, and they usually add little extra blood pressure control.\n\nWas this a typo, or is there a reason for 20 mg? Here are some common options:\n\n- **Amlodipine 10 mg PO once daily.** This is the max dose. Starting doses are usually 5 mg, or 2.5 mg for frail or elderly patients, and it's titrated up over 1\u20132 weeks.\n- **Amlodipine 5 mg PO once daily.** This is the standard starting dose.\n- **Adding a second agent.** If 10 mg isn't enough, adding an ACE inhibitor, ARB, or thiazide is generally preferred to going above 10 mg.\n\nTell me which dose you want. If you do intend 20 mg, please confirm that with your supervising resident or attending. I can enter it with a note saying it was reviewed."
      }
     ],
